[Music]  what I wanted to do is just talk a  little bit about some of the premises  behind the book that I published with  Imran our armored it's about the what I  think is a baleful effect of what is a  very hegemonic economic idea that I  think has been with us since well for  the last 40 years and this is the idea  which I refer to as market  fundamentalism a market fundamentalism  believes is a theory that you can  actually best distribute all decisions  goods issues fire market forces rather  than using government or other forms of  deliberation and that somehow the  outcome of a market in distributional  terms is by far the fairest and ensures  that people have the most Liberty that  they're going to have and this idea was  first generated and talked about by an  Austrian philosopher and economist  called Frederick Hayek and he wrote a  book in 1943 called the road to serfdom  so the title of this book is a play on  the road to serfdom because my thesis is  that far from avoiding serfdom as Hayek  wanted us to do by using the market to  distribute everything we've actually  created a new serfdom for ourselves by  taking his ideas to the extreme and  privilege in the market in a way a  fundamentalist way and giving it far  more power and giving it far more affect  and influence than it was actually  created to have and so the paradox is  we've ended up where he warned  that we would end up if we were  collective he wrote his book in 1943 at  the height of the effort to win the war  against Hitler in which he noticed that  the British state and the American state  to some extent had used state power and  collectivism to ensure that we had an  economy that worked effectively enough  to fight Hitler and to get a proper  outcome to the war and he felt that that  kind of collective action socialism if  we could use that word would actually  take away all people's liberty and that  they would not be free at all and they  would be dictated to and in fact his  view of democratic socialism and some of  what he worried about actually played  into Churchill's notorious Gestapo  speech in the 1945 election where he  said if the Labour Party won the  election and they'd in evitable II have  to create a kind of Gestapo to run  things and Clement Attlee being the  great leader that he was  put him down with the observation that  actually there were far more things to  worry about than the Labour Party  turning into a Gestapo such as fighting  poverty and ensuring that we dealt with  inequality so after a certain Margaret  Hilda Roberts got to Oxford she picked  up this book and it's fair to say that  it was the most influential book that  she ever read and she was very open  about this when she was Prime Minister  and in fact she was delighted to be  given a leather-bound copy of the Road  to Serfdom by the great man himself when  she was Prime Minister and there's  letters gushing letters between the two  of them talking about how they were  going to destroy the trade unions and  create near Varner and Britain so this  book really looks at what's happened to  our society since that kind of obsession  with market forces has come to the fore  and it's not really a very pretty sight  we've had we've got a very deregulated  labor market increasingly where we have  an hourglass kind of structure so some  people do very well at the lovely end of  the labor market but there's also a  lousy end and the lousy end is growing  and more and more people are falling  into the lousy end of the labor market  and it's particularly difficult for  people now if they're in employment to  earn a reasonable amount of money if  they're not in the lovely end and so you  see people who work very many hours  don't have trade union rights or  recognition so find it very difficult to  access employment rights who actually  are in poverty and 60 percent of those  in poverty now are actually in work so  even though people are doing the right  thing they're not being given a reward  and nor are they being able to set up  and start family lives or develop her  own homes aspire to have children and  all the things that we took for granted  and in fact increasingly what we're  seeing is people who are working longer  hours for Less people's who whose  earnings haven't recovered since the  crash in 2008 and in real terms are  still below who can't see that life is  going to get better for their children  and it was for them and that means that  we've got an increasing level of  pessimism and so some of what I think  we're seeing is the limitations of this  obsession with markets that Frederick  Hayek injected into our system that  Ronald Reagan made certain was injected  into the  American system and that mrs. Thatcher  left us with a legacy of and legacy that  we didn't manage to overturn when we  were last in government where we failed  to change the political terms of trade  enough to turn off this down escalator  into the lousy er ends of the labor  market and what the book really points  out is that these kinds of assumptions  where the price mechanism in a market is  somehow the only measurement of value  where if you're well-off and rich then  you need to be rewarded but if you're  poor you need to be made poorer so you  work harder where somehow morality is  upended so that if you're successful  that is it in itself and somehow and  being selfish at that end is a good  moral thing whereas if you're not then  then somehow there's something wrong  with you because you're not successful  all of these issues are the exact  opposite of the kind of morality and  value system that democratic socialism  would bring to the party and what I say  in the book is that there are five  things that Democratic socialists  believe that we need to reassert so  there are five values really to sum up  what we believe equality in democracy in  Liberty in cooperation not just  individualism not just being isolated  and have in a dog-eat-dog kind of world  but cooperation and internationalism and  I also say that a lot of the issues that  are facing us now are actually only  going to be solved if we cooperate  across borders and if we cooperate  rather than we're in competition so  terrorism for example doesn't know  borders and needs cooperation the  problems associated with climate change  and environmental sustainability  absolutely need cooperation and creating  a fairer more equal society isn't all of  our interests at home as well as it is  abroad and so some of the things I say  that we should be thinking of creating a  much more  all basis for a taxation system getting  away from this idea and this culture  that we have in the banking industry for  example at the moment I'll be gone and  you'll be gone as John Kay calls it you  can extract all the wealth you like you  can do all the dodgy things you like  because by the time they find out about  it I'll be gone and you'll be gone we'll  have taken our bonuses we'll live and  and and so somebody else can clear up  the mess and we don't care that's not  the kind of society that we need to  create that's not the kind of behavior  that we need to reward but we have been  rewarding it and we can't stop it unless  we take a much more collective view  about what's right and what's wrong than  what we want to incentivize I also say  that we need to have a much more  empowering government at the moment we  have had eight years of austerity been  coruscating for areas like the one I  represent up in Wallasey where we've  seen huge problems of poverty huge  issues and problems caused by lack of  access to food because of changes to  benefit systems the rise of food banks  and food poverty is an absolutely  scandal in a country like ours that  actually we need to be take much more  responsibility for the kind of society  we create and the kind of values that it  has for more debates talks and  interviews subscribe today to the  Institutes of Arts and ideas at AI TV  you

